    "agent_messages": [
      "I added `POST /purchases/bulk-reject` and kept bulk-approve working the same way. `go build`, `go vet` and `gofmt` pass. Nothing was tested against a real database: the credentials aren't available, and the repo has no test files, so `go test ./...` had nothing to run.\n\nWhat changed, all under `internal/candidate/`:\n- **`reject.go` (new):** defines the reject Action as moving a purchase from `submitted` to `rejected`, stored as operation `\"bulk-reject.v4\"`, with the response field `rejectedIds`. It also has one shared helper that calls the protected Action kernel and turns its errors into the HTTP adapter's error type.\n- **`approval.go`:** both `/purchases/bulk-approve` and `/purchases/bulk-reject` are now registered in `protocol.Routes` and use that helper. Bulk-approve calls the kernel exactly as before, so its behaviour doesn't change.\n\nThe rest of the requirements come from code that was already there; I read it but didn't run any of it:\n- **Shared HTTP adapter (`protocol.HTTP`):** checks the login and approver role, the body size limit and the Idempotency-Key header, and sorts and de-duplicates the IDs.\n- **Action kernel:**\n  - runs everything in one transaction and only picks targets in your tenant, created by someone else, with status `submitted`;\n  - rejects the whole batch with 409 if any target doesn't qualify;\n  - handles idempotency per operation, tenant, user and key, including replays;\n  - writes the `ActionRequest` row in the same transaction and returns 503 with a rollback if that write fails.\n- **Concurrency:** the kernel takes a lock per operation, tenant, user and key, and locks the target rows. A batch that loses a race finds targets no longer `submitted` and gets 409. That covers overlapping batches and an approve racing a reject on the same purchase.\n- **`SCHEMA.sql` (v4 section):** already allows the `rejected` status, the `bulk-reject.v4` operation, and an RLS update rule (`purchase_decide`) that only applies to `submitted` rows. Once a purchase is approved or rejected, it can't be approved or rejected again.\n\nThe audit table allows only one approve-or-reject record per purchase, which also blocks a second decision."
    ],
